# Missing Module Prevention

Every Python file referenced in `main.py` imports must exist on the production server at `/opt/clinical-sovereignty-lab/`. A missing file causes `No module named` and drops the service health count.

## Before Restarting Backend

After deploying code changes, verify no modules are missing:

```bash
ssh root@68.183.168.75 "docker logs nate_backend --since 30s 2>&1 | grep 'No module named' | sort -u"
```

If any appear, deploy the missing file:

```bash
scp backend/app/services/MODULE.py root@68.183.168.75:/opt/clinical-sovereignty-lab/backend/app/services/MODULE.py
```

## Common Miss Patterns

| Pattern | Why It Happens |
|---|---|
| New service created locally, only `main.py` deployed | Service file never reaches server |
| Service imports a helper module | Helper deployed, but service file not |
| `security/` subdirectory files | `__init__.py` or subdirectory doesn't exist on server |
| Router + service pair | Router deployed, service it imports not deployed |

## Transitive Dependency Chain

When deploying a new service, trace its imports:

```
main.py imports → service.py imports → helper.py imports → ...
```

ALL files in the chain must be on the server. A single missing link produces `No module named`.

